{"name":"Taste of Berry","hex":"#c8a3b8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"taste","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the sensations produced by the tongue in response to certain chemicals; the quality of giving this sensation.","The sense that consists in the perception and interpretation of this sensation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sample the flavor of something orally.","To have a taste; to excite a particular sensation by which flavor is distinguished."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/taste"},{"term":"berry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small succulent fruit, of any one of many varieties.","A soft fruit which develops from a single ovary and contains seeds not encased in pits."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To pick berries.","To bear or produce berries."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/berry"}],"entries":[{"title":"Taste","blocks":[{"text":"The gustatory system or sense of taste is the sensory system that is partially responsible for the perception of taste. Taste is the perception stimulated when a substance in the mouth reacts chemically with taste receptor cells located on taste buds in the oral cavity, mostly on the tongue. Taste, along with the sense of smell and trigeminal nerve stimulation, determines flavors of food and other substances. Humans have taste receptors on taste buds and other areas, including the upper surface of the tongue and the epiglottis. The gustatory cortex is responsible for the perception of taste."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Taste_bud.svg/330px-Taste_bud.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taste"},{"title":"Berry","blocks":[{"text":"A berry is a small, pulpy, and often edible fruit. Typically, berries are juicy, rounded, brightly colored, sweet, sour or tart, and do not have a stone or pit although many pips or seeds may be present.‍ Common examples of berries in the culinary sense are strawberries, raspberries, blueberries, blackberries, white currants, blackcurrants, and redcurrants.‍ In Britain, soft fruit is a horticultural term for such fruits.‍"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg/330px-Berberis_thunbergii_berries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/1402_The_Tongue.jpg/500px-1402_The_Tongue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/1402_The_Tongue.jpg/1280px-1402_The_Tongue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/1402_The_Tongue.jpg/500px-1402_The_Tongue.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taste buds and papillae of the human tongue","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1402_The_Tongue.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg/500px-Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg/1280px-Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg/500px-Jaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Taste receptors of the human tongue","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJaime_Lara_2023_PMID_36409650_Figure_3.jpg"},{"s